@design/agent-config.sigil from AgentConfig import {
  agent definition, tool list, thinking level,
}
@design/task-store.sigil from TaskStore import {
  task record, event log, parent session id,
}

component TaskCore {
  goal {
    Run each *task* from an agent definition and a brief on one child, keep it in a
    durable task record, and return one bounded *result*.
  }

  state {
    A task holds a *task id*, the frozen agent definition it was created from, a
    *brief* and one *child run*. A task id is sz- followed by six base32
    characters. The brief is the one self-contained text that the child receives,
    and the child sees nothing else of the parent conversation.

    A child run has a *child run state*. It is Pending until its caller
    releases it, then moves through Starting and Live to Settled, and it can
    also end Failed or Cancelled.

    A failed child run carries one *failure kind*, which is timeout, crashed, error
    or skill_mismatch. A cancelled child run carries no failure kind.

    A result has a status of completed, truncated, failed or cancelled. It holds
    the task id first, a *summary* and the *session usage*, and a failed result
    also holds its failure kind. The status truncated means only that the child
    stopped at its own length limit. The summary is cut to the *result budget*,
    fixed at 2000 tokens and counted by estimate.

    A session usage is the token count, the cost and the context use at settle of a
    child run, as its driver reports them. Each field is optional, and a field that
    the driver cannot report is unavailable and never zero.

    A *launch spec* is the record of how a driver started a child run, without its
    prompt: the command as one opaque list, the working directory, the names of its
    environment variables and never their values, the version of the child
    program, the skill folder paths and the thinking level that the driver set for
    the child run. A field that the driver cannot report is unavailable.

    A *domain event* is one step of a child run, such as run started, skills
    verified, tool activity, agent message, turn settled and run exited. Tool
    activity carries the tool name and its target path, and the run started event
    carries the launch spec of the child run. Each domain event is written to the
    event log of its task.
  }

  logic {
    On a task request, create the task with a new task id, the frozen
    agent definition and the brief, and write the task record with the status
    running. The task id goes to the result and to the child port, and the
    agent definition goes to the child port. The task record goes to the final
    step, which writes the result into it. Keep the child run Pending until its
    caller releases it. Check that the task holds exactly one brief, and pass
    that brief to the child port when the child run is released, which starts
    the child run and ends this part of the flow.

    While the child run is live, write each domain event to the event log and offer
    it to the caller, which ends that branch. When the run started event arrives,
    write the launch spec it carries into the task record without reading it, which
    ends that branch. When the child run settles, build the result with the task id
    first, the summary cut to the result budget and the session usage. The result
    goes to the final step. A failed child run builds its result with its
    failure kind, and a cancelled child run builds it with the status cancelled and
    no failure kind. Both go to the same final step. In the final step, write the
    result into the task record and return the result to the caller, which ends
    the flow.
  }

  constraints {
    A task has at most one live child run, and a task request produces exactly one
    result.

    TaskCore must reach a child only through the *child port* and must not import
    host-specific types. TaskCore requires the child port, which a child driver
    provides, and does not depend on any child driver.

    TaskCore requires the task record and depends on TaskStore, which provides
    it. Only TaskCore may ask TaskStore to write a task record, and TaskStore
    performs the write.

    The result must not carry the *transcript*, tool output or skill content of the
    child run.

    TaskCore must not record or compute which files a child run changed, must not
    snapshot or copy workspace files and must not gate writes. The child run is
    limited only by its tool list, and what it writes is for the user to review
    with git, including when a parent and a child write the same files.

    A cancelled child run that is still Pending must never start a child. It
    has the status cancelled, no failure kind, no session usage and no summary.

    A summary longer than the result budget must be cut keeping its head at a line
    boundary, with an open code fence closed, and must end with a visible marker
    that names the omitted amount and says that subzero show with the task id has
    the full text. The cut leaves the status unchanged, an empty summary stays
    empty, and a truncated result whose summary is over the result budget carries
    the marker as well.

    A field of the session usage that the child run cannot report must be marked
    unavailable and never reported as zero.

    A launch spec must never hold the value of an environment variable, because
    the task record is plain text, and TaskCore must write the launch spec into the
    task record unread and must not depend on any flag that it holds.

    TaskCore owns one brief for each task in the spike, so continue, ask, fork and
    rerun are not offered.
  }

  cases {
    A summary longer than the result budget is cut with a visible marker, the
    status stays completed, and the task id is still first in the result.

    A child run whose last assistant message has the stop reason error is Failed
    with the failure kind error, and a cancelled child run carries no failure kind.

    The run started event of a child run puts its launch spec in the task record
    before the child run settles.
  }

  interface {
    Accept a task request made of an agent definition, a brief and an optional
    parent session id. Return the result, which never carries the transcript, with
    the task id first once the child run settles, or let the caller leave the task
    running and ask for its result later. A child run stays in the
    child run state Pending until its caller releases it, so that the caller can
    limit how many child runs run at the same time.

    Cancel a live child run as cancelled, and return the result.

    Offer each domain event of a live child run to the caller as it happens, and
    offer a failure kind in the result of a failed child run.

    Define the child port that a child driver implements: start a child run for a
    task id, an agent definition and a brief, hand its launch spec to the caller in
    the run started domain event, run it to settlement, cancel it, and report the
    outcome, which is the last assistant text, the status, the failure kind of a
    failed run and the session usage.
  }

  decisions {
    The spike keeps one brief per task because each interpreter call is a fresh
    single request, and the experiment does not need follow-up turns. Cancelled is
    a status and not a failure kind, because a failure kind belongs to a failed
    result that a parent received. The launch spec is a shape of TaskCore and not
    of the pi driver, so that TaskCore, TaskStore and Cli carry no pi type. The
    result budget is fixed because an
    agent definition has no field for another value, and TaskCore keeps no list of
    changed files because git is the record and the review of concurrent writers is
    the user's.
  }
}
